{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>Make me know, I pray, Your ways.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): 'Your ways' means Your attributes, for the ways of HaShem are His attributes. Thus David, peace be upon him, explained (Psalms 103:7): ״He made known His ways to Moses,״ and he immediately mentioned the attributes: רחום וחנון ה׳ ארך אפים ורב חסד. Now Moses asked for knowledge of HaShem through His ways and His attributes, that is, through the ways of His actions, and HaShem answered him: ״I will cause all My goodness to pass before your face.״ And Moses asked two things: one possible, which is ״make me know Your ways,״ and one impossible, which is ״show me Your glory.״ And to the request ״show me,״ the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״You shall not be able to see My face.״ And concerning these two, Solomon said in his wisdom (Proverbs 25:2): ״The glory of Elohim hides a thing, and the glory of kings searches out a thing.״ He explained that the knowledge of HaShem falls into two parts: one part is impossible, and that is what he said, ״The glory of Elohim hides a thing״; and the second part is possible, and a person can investigate it, and that is ״and the glory of kings searches out a thing,״ that is, investigation of His kingship from the aspect of His actions. Thus the prophet, peace be upon him, said (Jeremiah 9:23): ״But let him who boasts boast in this: understanding and knowing Me, for I am YHWH who does kindness, justice, and righteousness in the earth.״ He explained that the knowledge of HaShem is knowledge of His attributes, so that a person recognizes His wisdom — how He governs His world through them. This is what the verse says: ״For I am YHWH who does kindness, justice, and righteousness in the earth,״ etc. For there is no device in the world to recognize Him, may He be exalted, from the aspect of the essence of His glory, only from the aspect of His actions, because He is farther than all that is far from the aspect of the essence of His glory, and He is closer than all that is close from the aspect of His ways and His actions. And the parable for this is from the sun: if one seeks to grasp it from the aspect of its actions, that is, its shining and its light illuminating the earth and its inhabitants, then one will apprehend its existence and make use of its light and attain the desired goal from it; but if one labors to grasp it from the aspect of its sphere and attempts to look with his eyes directly into the eye of the sun, his eyes will be blinded and he will derive no benefit from the sun. So too in the apprehension of HaShem: from the aspect of His actions, that is, from the aspect of His wisdom and His power in all His creatures, we will comprehend and understand His matter, and then our intellect will be illuminated; but when we labor regarding the essence of His glory, likening Him and imagining Him in our thought — lo, we blind the eyes of our intellect from apprehending anything of Him. And of this it is said: ״The glory of Elohim hides a thing.״" "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'Make me know Your ways' means His attributes, that is, His actions, and they are the existent beings of Genesis, through which He, blessed be He, is described, for through them did man know Him; and this is what he said, ״that I may know You,״ and the one who knows Him, may He be exalted, is the one who finds favor — this is what he said, ״so that I may find favor in Your eyes.״ And included in His attributes, which are His actions, are the actions proceeding from Him, blessed be He, in the world, and they are the attributes that the Creator, may He be exalted, mentioned to him. And therefore He said, <b>and see that this nation is with You,</b> that is, that I need to govern them through these actions, through the ways of Your actions. And He answered him: 'My face will go' — from the language of anger, in the manner of (I Samuel 1:18) ״and her face was no longer hers,״ that is, her face showed no more anger." "<b>Now,</b> Moses asked for a possible apprehension, which is knowledge of the attributes, and he also asked for an impossible apprehension when he said, ״Show me,״ which is a request for His essence and His truth, blessed be He. And the Creator, may He be exalted, answered him regarding the request ״make me know Your ways,״ which is knowledge of His attributes that are His actions: ״I will cause all My goodness to pass.״ And this goodness is what He showed him — that He would let him see His actions, which are all the existent beings, concerning which it is written (Genesis 1:31): ״And Elohim saw all that He had made, and behold, it was very good,״ that is, that he would grasp their nature and the interconnection of some of them with others, and know His governance of them, how it is in general and in particular. And regarding the request ״show me,״ He answered him: ״You shall not be able to see My face,״ that is, the truth of My essence, ״for no man shall see Me and live.״ But ״behold, there is a place with Me,״ that is, there is a place of reflection from which you will apprehend the utmost of what it is possible for man to apprehend. And what He said, ״and you shall see My back,״ means all the existent beings; and He called them 'back,' as one who casts a thing behind him. And this parable was lifted up to say that their existence is far from His existence, may He be exalted. From this Onkelos translated: ״ית דבתראי״ — that is, all the existent beings, which are behind Him and subordinate to Him. And what He said, ״and My face shall not be seen,״ means the truth of His essence — that it can never be apprehended in any way. And according to Onkelos's translation, ״ודי קדמאי,״ it alludes to the existence of mighty created beings that are impossible to apprehend in any way as they truly are, and they are the separate intelligences (i.e., the angels), and He attributed them to the Creator, blessed be He, to say that they are before Him and constantly in His hands, because of the strength of His providence over them at all times." "<b>Now,</b> He withheld from him that apprehension called 'face' and promised him an apprehension lower than it, which is called 'back.' And in order to explain that this apprehension is impossible, He said: ״And YHWH passed before his face.״ The word העברה in this place is like the language of (I Samuel 20:36) ״and he shot the arrow to cause it to pass (להעבירו),״ which is a borrowed term for one who causes one intent to miss and aims at a different intent and a different goal. And the words ״before his face״ are a reference to the Creator, may He be exalted. The meaning of the verse is that the Creator, may He be exalted, concealed from him that apprehension called 'face' that he asked about in saying ״show me Your glory,״ for from within the reply that was given — ״and My face shall not be seen״ — it is clear that the apprehension of the 'face' was what he sought from Him; and therefore He said ״and YHWH passed before his face״ — He concealed from him and diverted his knowledge from that apprehension called 'face' and redirected it to another matter, which is knowledge of the attributes, that is, the actions proceeding from Him, blessed be He, which are the thirteen attributes that He mentioned — and this is the request he made first: ״make me know Your ways.״ And according to the opinion of Onkelos, who translated ״ואעבר — the Shekhinah of YHWH,״ the thing that passed was created glory without doubt, and it is the Shekhinah, which is created glory; and the verse ״and it shall be while My glory passes״ supports his opinion. And ״before his face״ refers back to Moses, meaning 'before him,' as in (Genesis 32:22) ״and the gift passed before his face (על פניו).״ Or ״and YHWH passed before his face״ may be as if it said: ״and the voice of YHWH passed before his face,״ for we find the language of passing used of a voice (Exodus 36:6): ״and they caused a voice to pass through the camp,״ and the word ״and He called״ would refer back to 'voice,' and the doubling ״YHWH, YHWH״ is like (Genesis 22:11) ״Abraham, Abraham,״ ״Moses, Moses.״ This is the opinion of the Rambam in his honored book the Guide, and this is his language: 'It is upon you to choose whichever of the opinions you wish — whether this entire great station was entirely a prophetic vision without doubt, and all the apprehensions were apprehensions that he sought or that were withheld from him and that which he attained were all intellectual, with no sensory element, as he explained there; or whether there was there, together with intellectual apprehension, a sensory apprehension of sight, but the perfection of intellectual apprehension would reach him through the vision of a created thing, as Onkelos wrote — this if that apprehension of the vision was not also a prophetic vision, like what appeared to Abraham (Genesis 15:17): ״and behold, a smoking furnace and a flaming torch that passed between the pieces״; or whether there was also an apprehension of the sense of hearing, and the voice that passed before his face was also the Creator without doubt. Choose whichever of the opinions you wish, for the intention in all of them is that you not believe the word ״passed״ in the manner of (Exodus 17:5) ״pass before the people,״ for YHWH, may He be exalted, is not a body and motion is not applicable to Him, and it is impossible to say that He passed according to the primary assumption of the language.' Thus far his language, of blessed memory, in Part I of his book, chapter 21." "<b>And the meaning of</b> these attributes that are ascribed to El, may He be exalted, is not that He, may He be exalted, undergoes passion through them, like a man's compassion for his fellow or a father's compassion for his son — for these cannot come except after passion and the stirring of compassion. But this benefit through the attribute of mercy comes from Him, blessed be He, without passion or change. And therefore He is called רחום וחנון, for when a person gives to one who has no claim upon him, he is called on that account חנון, as in (Job 19:21): ״חנוני חנוני אתם רעי — Show pity upon me, show pity upon me, my friends.״ So too the Creator, may He be exalted, in that He bestows upon one who has no claim upon Him, is called חנון, and the same reasoning applies to the rest of the attributes, which are all attributes of mercy. This opinion is the opinion of the Rambam, of blessed memory, and lengthy is his language." "<b>And the Gaon</b> Rabbenu Saadiah explained: you cannot see the face — the beginning of the light; and you shall see the back — the end of the light, but its beginning you cannot see. And it is possible that upon this he said (Ezekiel 1:27): ״וארא כעין חשמל כמראה אש בית לה סביב ממראה מתניו ולמעלה״ — this is the beginning; and ״ממראה מתניו ולמטה ראיתי כמראה אש״ — this is the end. And upon this the verse says, according to this interpretation (Habakkuk 3:5): ״לפניו ילך דבר ויצא רשף לרגליו״ — explaining that לפניו, which is the beginning, ילך דבר, that is, that no man can see Him and live; but רגליו, which is the end, goes out like רשף, as Ezekiel said: ״ראיתי כמראה אש״, for this is possible. And so it was said to Moses: ״וראית את אחורי״. And this is what is written (Psalms 50:3): ״אש לפניו תאכל וסביביו נשערה מאד״ — that is, לפניו אש תאכל, but סביביו — it is possible that storm should come upon a man." "<b>By way of the Kabbalah:</b> הודיעני נא את דרכך — the path of goodness by which You guide Your worlds, and through which You are known — they are the ways of צדיק יסוד עולם, of whom it is written (Psalms 145:17): ״צדיק ה' בכל דרכיו״. And these ways that Moses asked for are the very ones which King Solomon, peace be upon him, mentioned as being too wondrous for him, as he said (Proverbs 30:18–19): ״שלשה המה נפלאו ממני וארבעה לא ידעתים דרך הנשר בשמים דרך נחש עלי צור דרך אניה בלב ים ודרך גבר בעלמה״. And Solomon meant that the action of צדיק יסוד עולם in this physical world is wondrous to him, and therefore he mentioned the air and the water and the human being, for this comprises the entirety of the lower world: that birds fly in the air, and animals tread upon the dust, and those who go down to the sea ride in ships, and the human species, which is male and female — this is ״דרך גבר בעלמה״. And he indicated that all of this is the way of the world and its custom; but what is wondrous to him is the governance — in what manner it is — and what the essence of the action of the צדיק is. And this matter, wondrous to Solomon, is what Moses now asked for, saying <b>that I may know You,</b> that I may know to declare Your unity. <b>That I may find grace in Your sight —</b> and He teaches you here that the one who knows to declare the unity of HaShem, blessed be He, is the one who finds grace in His eyes. <b>And consider that this nation is with You</b> — whom I am guiding, and therefore I will need You to inform me of this. The Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: ״פני ילכו״ — this is the emissary, Metatron, through whom I am known in the world through awesome deeds and great wonders, in signs and portents that will be performed through him, by means of which the creatures will know Me and see Me, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 14:31): ״וירא ישראל את היד הגדולה אשר עשה ה' במצרים וייראו העם את ה' ויאמינו בה'״. והנחותי לך — from the language of נחת רוח, that is, I will rest him with you so that he will not conduct himself with you in the strict measure of judgment but in a weakened measure of judgment intermingled with mercy. And this emissary is the Glory called Shekhinah, and therefore Onkelos translated: ״שכינתי תהך״. And Moses was not satisfied with him, because he is the measure of judgment, and therefore he replied: ״אם אין פניך הולכים אל תעלנו מזה״ — if Your face does not go itself, in Your glory, this is the measure of Tif'eret and Glory — do not bring us up from here, for with these two measures You brought them out of Egypt, as it is said: בכח גדול וביד חזקה — not ביד חזקה alone. And so did the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expound: in the redemption from Egypt, not by the hand of an angel, and not by the hand of a seraph, and not by the hand of an emissary — which is Metatron — but the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself and in His Glory — that is, Tif'eret and Glory. And the Holy One, blessed be He, acknowledged him in this: ״גם את הדבר הזה אשר דברת אעשה״, and the word גם teaches, as if He said: the thing that I spoke to you — ״פני ילכו״ — גם את הדבר אשר דברת — that thing too, I will do. Once He acknowledged him in this and he found a time of favor, he asked for more than this and said: ״הראני נא את כבודך״ — and that is the Keter, and he was not answered in this; and He answered him: ״לא תוכל לראות את פני״, and Onkelos translated: ״אפי שכינתי״ — because the Shekhinah plants her house at the end of the Atzilut, therefore He called the beginning of the Atzilut ״פנים״, for the beginning of everything and its highest part is called its ״face״. And therefore He answered him: ״לא תוכל לראות את פני״ — that is, the supernal face, the face of the Atzilut. But ״הנה מקום אתי״, and ״וראית את אחורי״ — that is the end of the Atzilut, which is what He had already acknowledged to him: ״אני אעביר כל טובי״. And the word אחורי is from the sense of אחור, which is west, as in (Psalms 139:5): ״אחור וקדם צרתני״. And it is known that the Shekhinah is in the west, and ״פני״ — the beginning of the Atzilut — will not be seen. And since Moses had already grasped the Glory and the Tif'eret, the verse says — and this is the more correct reading in connecting the matter — ״הנה מקום אתי״: I am giving you a place of supernal apprehension, that you may grasp the Sefirah called חכמה; this is ״וראית את אחורי״, and the alef will be added, from the language of חיור, for חכמה is called לבנון. And the addition is not for nothing, but to teach that it is first with regard to our apprehension. And upon this the masters of truth hinted in saying: He showed him the knot of tefillin — that is, the tefillin of the head, which is in the shape of a dalet. And it is known that ״ד׳ יסודה חכמה״, for so it is said in the Book of Yetzirah concerning בג״ד כפר״ת. And Solomon hinted at her (Song of Songs 7:6): ״ודלת ראשך כארגמן״. But the Keter cannot be grasped — this is ״ופני לא יראו״. And then ״וירד ה' בענן״, and the language of ירידה here is apprehension, like those who ״descend״ to the Chariot. And the meaning of the verse is that Moses grasped the Unique Name through the Glory that comes in the cloud. ״ויתיצב עמו שם״ — who stood there with him? The Glory, which is called שם, in the language of (Genesis 2:10): ״ומשם יפרד״, for from there downward is the world of the Nifradim. And it is also called שם (II Samuel 6:2): ״להעלות משם את ארון האלהים אשר נקרא שם״. And so Moses mentioned above: ״ואתה אמרת ידעתיך בשם״, and he said ״ויקרא בשם ה'״ — meaning, the Glory is called by the Name of YHWH. And the word בשם is attached to the Unique Name, and so Onkelos translated: בשמא דה'. ״ויעבור ה'״ — this is the Glory, and in order to fulfill for him ״אני אעביר כל טובי״. And what He said, ״על פניו״, the intent is upon the face of Moses. And since this Glory that passed is the Shekhinah, therefore Onkelos translated: ״ואעביר ה' שכינתיה על אפוהי״. And the word ״ויקרא״ returns to the Glory, for it is the one that proclaims the thirteen middot, which are in the measure of Tif'eret — it is the Unique Name of YHWH, the measure of mercy, for you will find all thirteen middot are merciful. And therefore the Sages call them מכילן דרחמי, attributing them all to the measure of Tif'eret, which is mercy." "<b>And behold,</b> Moses our teacher, after he learned the orders of prayer and supplication, bowed to the One who passed before him and asked before Him that He walk always in their midst — as it is said: ״אם נא מצאתי חן בעיניך״ — in the measure of judgment — ״ילך נא אדני״ — this is the Shekhinah, and so Onkelos translated: ״תהך כען שכינתא דה' ביננא״. And this prayer of Moses is that he asked that the matter be so always from here onward, that the Shekhinah walk in their midst in every place they may be, because they are a stiff-necked people and will need this, for it is better for them with the Shekhinah, which is a weakened measure of judgment, more than with the angel that He mentioned to him above: ״ושלחתי לפניך מלאך״, by which the intent is an angel of the Nifradim. And behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, acknowledged him in this and promised him in the two measures: that He would settle the Shekhinah in the midst of the people, and that His Tif'eret would be with Moses. That He would settle the Shekhinah in the midst of the people — this is what He said to him: ״הנה אנכי כורת ברית״ — this is the angel of the covenant, and it is the Shekhinah. And He said: ״נגד כל עמך אעשה נפלאות אשר לא נבראו בכל הארץ ובכל הגוים וראה כל העם אשר אתה בקרבו״. And that His Tif'eret would be with Moses — this is what He said: ״את מעשה ה' כי נורא הוא״, and it would have been fitting to say ״את מעשי ה' כי נוראים הם״, but the intent is toward the Tif'eret, which is mercy, and therefore He said: ״את מעשה ה'״. And He said: ״אשר אני עושה עמך״ — this is a promise that He will be with him in the measure of mercy. From now on, Moses and the children of Israel are promised in this parashah in the measure of Tif'eret and Glory, for so he asked of Him, exalted be He: ״ונפלינו אני ועמך״ — אני in the measure of Tif'eret, and עמך in the Shekhinah. And behold, the entire parashah is fully explained. And I have already mentioned in the section of וישמע יתרו, on the verse ״וידבר אלהים״, that Moses, peace be upon him, grasped seven Sefirot, and they are the seven voices hinted at there in the parashah, and there I explained everything. And when he asked ״הראני נא את כבודך״, above the seven, he asked for what is the face of the Atzilut, and it was not given to him — and this is what is written: ״לא תוכל לראות את פני״. And such is the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, in his Kabbalah." "<b>But</b> the opinion of the sage R. Abraham, of blessed memory: ״פני ילכו״ — this angel, he is Metatron; פנים — faces of wrath. ״אם אין פניך״ — faces of mercy; this is the Tif'eret, which pardons and forgives transgressions. And He acknowledged him in this: ״גם את הדבר אשר דברת אעשה״. Once He acknowledged him, he turned back and asked for more than this: ״הראני נא את כבודך״, and these are the Keter and the Ḥesed and the Tif'eret. And all this so that he not return empty from his prayer — for if he is not answered through the one, he may be answered through the one above him. And then he was answered: ״לא תוכל לראות את פני״, and in this explanation, the beginning of the middot. And according to his opinion, Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, did not grasp from Tif'eret and above, but grasped only five Sefirot alone. And this is the opinion of the sage, of blessed memory. But the principle is what we have received, which is the opinion of the Rav, of blessed memory, and I have already mentioned it as explained on the verse ״וידבר״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {8 "R. Abraham here is identified by the contrast with 'the Rav' (Ramban) and by the specific view that Moses grasped only five Sefirot; the reference is to R. Abraham ibn Ezra."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:08:00.867376328Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}